# Root-only crash/hang diagnostics -- applied to EVERY profile, release included.
#
# Design goal (the "dmesg model"): keep useful kernel debugging permanently
# compiled in, but reachable ONLY by root. Everything here surfaces through the
# kernel ring buffer, which is dmesg_restrict=1 and SELinux-gated, so an
# untrusted app sees nothing; `dmesg` via su shows it all. /proc/config.gz is
# redacted (verify-image.sh), so these options don't advertise themselves either.
#
# Scope note on the Duck Detector (com.eltavine.duckdetector) crash that
# motivated this file: it turned out to be a genuine panic() that cold-resets the
# phone, NOT a lockup and NOT a recoverable oops -- the soft/hard-lockup
# detectors below never fired for it, and it reboots regardless of PANIC_ON_OOPS.
# It is also un-capturable on this device: LG's bootloader wipes the ramoops RAM
# on every reset, lge.crash_handler/lge.hiddenreset are bootloader params we
# cannot override, and 4.9 netconsole is impossible here (no ndo_poll_controller
# on wlan0 or the u_ether gadget). That investigation is closed and handed to
# upstream -- see tests/baselines/duckdetector-kernel-panic-UPSTREAM-REPORT.md
# and [[duckdetector-kernel-panic-capture]]. The detectors below are kept not for
# that bug but as general, root-only aids for future hook development.

# Soft/hard-lockup detector, WARN-only. A CPU spinning in-kernel (a hook stuck on
# a lock, a deadlock) prints "BUG: soft lockup - CPU#N stuck ..." plus a full call
# trace to the ring while the other CPUs keep running -- long enough to read it
# live with `dmesg`. Panic-on-lockup is deliberately left OFF: a panic reboots,
# and on this device a reboot destroys the ring (ramoops is wiped), so panicking
# would trade the trace for nothing. It stays runtime-armable if ever wanted
# (echo 1 > /proc/sys/kernel/softlockup_panic).
CONFIG_LOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
# CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC is not set

# Hung-task detection, also WARN-only. Panic-on-hang is off so a 120s D-state
# false positive (slow storage, a boot stall) can never bootloop the daily
# kernel; root can arm it on demand (echo 1 > /proc/sys/kernel/hung_task_panic).
CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_HUNG_TASK_TIMEOUT=120
# CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC is not set

# --- Oops handling: FAIL CLOSED ----------------------------------------------
# An oops means kernel state is corrupted. On a root-hiding kernel, letting it
# keep running is both a security and an anti-detection risk:
#   - a corrupted-state window is exactly what a privilege-escalation exploit
#     wants; failing stop (panic) closes it. This is why Android hardens to
#     panic_on_oops=1.
#   - if a SuSFS/KSU hook faults, the hiding can end up half-applied while the
#     rest of Android keeps running -- leaving the REAL root files exposed to a
#     detector. A clean crash prevents any app from seeing that unhidden state.
# So the shipped kernel (baseline and release) fails closed.
#
# Caveat specific to THIS device: panic_on_oops does NOT buy a crash log here,
# because the LG bootloader wipes the ramoops/pstore RAM on every reset (see the
# header) -- a panic-reboot leaves pstore empty. So when you are ACTIVELY
# developing hooks and need the backtrace, build --profile=debug, which flips
# this back OFF so the kernel survives the fault and you read the trace live with
# `dmesg` before the watchdog resets. That live read is the only capture that
# works on this phone, and that build is never shipped.
CONFIG_PANIC_ON_OOPS=y
